Each node corresponds to a rule. If the rule has a name, the name is printed, otherwise the whole rule is formated. Every optional appears, but it can be <empty> (that way the analyzer will always see the sub rules at the same place). And repetitions add another level (note that the both the whole repetition and each occurence is printed with the same rule). Anyway, that's a lot of fun.

Now the difficult part is providing nice error messages to the user. At the moment, two kinds of errors can occur.
1. When parsing a rule, if the lookahead token is not in the rule's first set.

for in list do

will produce the following message:

Parser error: example.sool:5: failed to parse list of names: expected 'Name', found 'in' instead
        for in list do
            ^^         
Parser info: list of names := 'Name' { ',' 'Name' }
Parser info: example.sool:5: in for loop started here:
        for in list do
        ^^^            
Compiler info: parser reported 1 error

That is, the list of possible tokens it expected, a reminder of the rule that it's trying to apply, and some context. The whole first set is given:

for a,b,c in + do

produces:

Parser error: example.sool:5: failed to parse list of expressions: expected one of 'Number' 'String' '#' 'Symbol' 'not' 'false' 'true' '{' 'Name' '(' '[' 'ClassName' '-' '<' '/' 'nil', found '+' instead
        for a,b,c in + do
                     ^    
Parser info: list of expressions := "expression" { ',' "expression" }
Parser info: example.sool:5: in for loop started here:
        for a,b,c in + do
        ^^^               
Compiler info: parser reported 1 error


2. When matching a terminal, a slightly different message is given: there can be only one token, and there is no rule reminder:

for a,b,c  list do

produces:

Parser error: example.sool:5: expected 'in', found 'Name' instead
        for a,b,c  list do
                   ^^^^    
Parser info: example.sool:5: in for loop started here:
        for a,b,c  list do
        ^^^                
Compiler info: parser reported 1 error

In the grammar, you can add a "name" field to a rule. The idea is:

1. make rule printing nicer when the actual rule fails. It says "failed to parse list of expressions" instead of "failed to parse "expression" { ',' "expression" }". If the rule has a name, the rule "content" is still reminded, but the first line is more clear. This is why the name should be "in context". The user does not care only about *what* should be parsed, but also *why*. The return types in a method definition, for instance, are concretely a "list of types". But the user wants to read "return types". So the rule is nameless:

g.list_type = {
    'type',
    { repetition = true, ',', 'type' }
}

and its reference from the method definition encloses it into a named one:

g.method_def = {
    name = 'method definition',
    'meth',
    { optional = true, 'private' },
    { name = 'return types', optional = true, 'list_type' },
    etc...

2. When an error occurs, it goes back up the call stack until it finds a rule with a name, to give context.

By cleverly giving rules the proper names (and when needed, making "dummy" rules only to give contextual information), we end up having something relatively clear, and more important, not hard coded.

The last user-friendly feature I want to add is reminding the last optionals that failed to parse before the current error. For instance, suppose I forget to put a type before a member name:

class Root
    foobar
end

generates at the moment:

Parser error: example.sool:2: expected 'end', found 'Name' instead
    foobar
    ^^^^^^ 
Parser info: example.sool:1: in class definition started here:
class Root
^^^^^      
Compiler info: parser reported 1 error

Indeed, it tried (and failed) to parse members and methods, but since these are optional (in a repetition), it didn't complain. The next token it wants is 'end'. But it would be nicer to provide the user with a reminder that before 'end', there are a few optional rules that would have worked too.

[Discontinuity]

Done! The parser now remembers every optional/repetition rule that it didn't apply, in order, and outputs the list when an error occurs. In the previous example, it would say: "expected 'end', method definition or member definition, found 'Name' instead". That's more user friendly. The list is simply emptied each time a token is consumed. That way we only have the last consecutive failed optionals.
